<header class="pa-intro fr-wrap fr-section"><p class="pa-breadcrumb"><a href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/prayerresources/' ) ); ?>">Resources</a> / Praying with children</p><div class="pa-intro-layout"><div class="pa-intro-story"><h1>Let prayer grow <br/> with your family.</h1><div class="pa-intro-copy"><p class="fr-lead">A small prayer plan for the people beside you.</p><p>Begin with your child’s interests, questions and needs. Choose a starting point, share a prayer, and carry one small act of care into the day. Try it together, right here.</p></div><a class="fr-primary" href="#prayer-plan">Find your prayer plan</a></div><figure class="pa-family-image"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_theme_file_uri( 'assets/photos/family-children-prayer.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Children of different ages gather with their family in prayer at The Father Peyton Center."/><figcaption>Family prayer at The Father Peyton Center.</figcaption></figure></div></header>
<section id="prayer-plan" class="pa-plan" aria-labelledby="pa-plan-title"><div class="fr-wrap fr-section"><h2 id="pa-plan-title">How will you pray today?</h2><p>Choose the approach that fits. Ages are starting points; listening quietly is always a way to take part.</p>
<nav class="pa-tabs" data-age-tabs aria-label="Choose an age group"><a id="pa-tab-babies" href="#pa-babies" data-age="babies">Babies &amp; preschoolers</a><a id="pa-tab-primary" href="#pa-primary" data-age="primary">Primary school</a><a id="pa-tab-preteens" href="#pa-preteens" data-age="preteens">Preteens</a><a id="pa-tab-teens" href="#pa-teens" data-age="teens">Teenagers</a></nav>
<section id="pa-babies" class="pa-age" data-age-panel="babies" aria-labelledby="pa-babies-title"><div class="pa-age-intro"><h3 id="pa-babies-title">A little moment of thanks.</h3><p>Let a familiar voice lead. A young child can point or answer; a baby can simply be with you.</p><button type="button" class="pa-follow" data-plan-start hidden>Follow this prayer together</button></div><div class="pa-activity"><ol class="pa-steps"><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Notice someone you love.</h4><p>Sit together and think of someone who has cared for your family. An adult can name one kindness. Invite a child to point to a family picture, say a name, or listen.</p></li><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Say a little thank you.</h4><p>Read this short family prayer slowly. A child may join in with “Amen.”</p><p class="pa-prayer">Jesus, thank you for the people who care for us. Help us share your love today. Amen.</p></li><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Carry the thanks with you.</h4><p>Offer a smile, a gentle word or a thank you to someone nearby. You can end here and pray again another day.</p></li></ol><div class="pa-plan-controls" data-plan-controls hidden><p data-plan-progress role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><div><button type="button" data-plan-back>Previous</button><button type="button" class="fr-primary" data-plan-next>Next</button></div><button type="button" class="pa-text-button" data-plan-restart>Start this prayer again</button><button type="button" class="pa-text-button" data-plan-all>See the whole activity</button></div></div></section>
<section id="pa-primary" class="pa-age" data-age-panel="primary" aria-labelledby="pa-primary-title"><div class="pa-age-intro"><h3 id="pa-primary-title">Pray for someone. <br/> Then show you care.</h3><p>A child who notices another person’s needs can bring that care into prayer. Let them choose a response or lead a familiar line.</p><button type="button" class="pa-follow" data-plan-start hidden>Follow this prayer together</button></div><div class="pa-activity"><ol class="pa-steps"><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Choose someone to pray for.</h4><p>Ask: “Who could use a little care today?” An adult can offer an example. Anyone may name a person or keep the intention quietly; no one needs to explain private details.</p></li><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Pray the Our Father.</h4><p>One person can lead while others join in or listen. Keep the words here for anyone learning.</p><p class="pa-prayer">Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name; thy kingdom come; thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven. Give us this day our daily bread; and forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us; and lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil. Amen.</p></li><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Choose one act of kindness.</h4><p>Could you help with a task, draw a picture, or share an encouraging word? Choose something small that fits your day. Offer it for the person you remembered.</p></li></ol><div class="pa-plan-controls" data-plan-controls hidden><p data-plan-progress role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><div><button type="button" data-plan-back>Previous</button><button type="button" class="fr-primary" data-plan-next>Next</button></div><button type="button" class="pa-text-button" data-plan-restart>Start this prayer again</button><button type="button" class="pa-text-button" data-plan-all>See the whole activity</button></div></div></section>
<section id="pa-preteens" class="pa-age" data-age-panel="preteens" aria-labelledby="pa-preteens-title"><div class="pa-age-intro"><h3 id="pa-preteens-title">Read. Wonder. <br/> Bring a question to prayer.</h3><p>Take turns reading a story of faith. Let curiosity lead the conversation; there is room for questions as well as answers.</p><button type="button" class="pa-follow" data-plan-start hidden>Follow this prayer together</button></div><div class="pa-activity"><ol class="pa-steps"><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Read about Mary together.</h4><p class="pa-reading">An angel brought Mary the news that she would become the mother of Jesus. Mary listened and asked how this could happen. After hearing the angel’s answer, she accepted what God asked of her.</p><p class="pa-reading-note">A short retelling of the Annunciation in Luke 1:26–38.</p><p>Take turns telling each other two or three things you noticed. What would you like to ask about this story? You can also use a story of a saint from a book you already have.</p></li><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Pray a Hail Mary.</h4><p>Bring your question or intention into prayer, aloud or quietly.</p><p class="pa-prayer">Hail Mary, full of grace, the Lord is with thee. Blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb, Jesus. Holy Mary, Mother of God, pray for us sinners, now and at the hour of our death. Amen.</p></li><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Keep the conversation open.</h4><p>Each person may choose one thought to carry into the day. If a question needs more time, agree to return to it together. You do not have to settle everything before you finish praying.</p></li></ol><div class="pa-plan-controls" data-plan-controls hidden><p data-plan-progress role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><div><button type="button" data-plan-back>Previous</button><button type="button" class="fr-primary" data-plan-next>Next</button></div><button type="button" class="pa-text-button" data-plan-restart>Start this prayer again</button><button type="button" class="pa-text-button" data-plan-all>See the whole activity</button></div></div></section>
<section id="pa-teens" class="pa-age" data-age-panel="teens" aria-labelledby="pa-teens-title"><div class="pa-age-intro"><h3 id="pa-teens-title">Bring your real day <br/> into prayer.</h3><p>Offer choice, make room to listen, and let an adult model prayer too. No one has to make a speech or share something personal.</p><button type="button" class="pa-follow" data-plan-start hidden>Follow this prayer together</button></div><div class="pa-activity"><ol class="pa-steps"><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Pause with one part of your day.</h4><p>Think of a friendship, responsibility, hope or difficult moment. Anyone may share an intention or hold it quietly. An adult can begin by naming something they are grateful for or need help with.</p></li><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Offer a short prayer.</h4><p>Choose someone to read these words, or take a quiet moment before praying together.</p><p class="pa-prayer">Lord Jesus, be with us in the things we face today. Help us listen, act with courage and treat others with kindness. Guide us when we do not know the next step. Amen.</p></li><li data-plan-step><h4 tabindex="-1">Choose a response.</h4><p>What is one small thing you can do with care today? It might be listening, asking for help, making an apology or following through on a responsibility. Keep your choice private if you prefer.</p></li></ol><div class="pa-plan-controls" data-plan-controls hidden><p data-plan-progress role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><div><button type="button" data-plan-back>Previous</button><button type="button" class="fr-primary" data-plan-next>Next</button></div><button type="button" class="pa-text-button" data-plan-restart>Start this prayer again</button><button type="button" class="pa-text-button" data-plan-all>See the whole activity</button></div></div></section>
<noscript><p>All four activities are shown above. Follow the three parts at your own pace.</p></noscript>
</div></section>
<section class="pa-mixed fr-wrap fr-section"><h2>Different ages. <br/> One shared moment.</h2><div><p>Let a younger child point or answer “Amen.” A reader can lead the words, an older child can choose a mystery, and an adult can listen or offer an intention. You do not all need the same role.</p><p>When you want to continue, the <a class="text-link" href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/how-to-pray-the-rosary/#guided-prayer' ) ); ?>">guided Rosary</a> keeps the complete words beside you. Children who enjoy tracing and coloring can try the <a class="text-link" href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/family-rosary-companion/' ) ); ?>">Family Rosary Companion</a>.</p></div></section>
<section class="pa-source fr-wrap"><p>Inspired by Tiffany Walsh’s reflection <cite>Navigating Prayer Time as Children Grow</cite>, published in Family Rosary’s original guide. Practical activities inspired by that invitation. <a href="https://275132.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hubfs/275132/Navigating%20Prayer%20Time%20as%20Children%20Grow-1.pdf">Read the original reflection (PDF)</a>.</p></section>
